4. Feature Engineering

I learned that raw measurements do not always provide the most useful representation of subscriber behaviour.

The exercise required creating at least four engineered features.

Tenure Band

I created a categorical tenure_band feature:

df['tenure_band'] = pd.cut(
    df['tenure_months'],
    bins=[0, 6, 18, 36, 72],
    labels=['0-6m', '7-18m', '19-36m', '37m+']
)

This groups subscribers according to their tenure.

I learned that banding can be useful when the relationship between a variable and revenue may not be purely linear.

Data per Voice

I created:

df['data_per_voice'] = (
    df['avg_monthly_gb'] /
    df['avg_voice_min'].clip(lower=1)
)

This creates a ratio between data usage and voice usage.

It provides a different representation of subscriber behaviour than looking at each usage value independently.

Complaints per Year

I created:

df['complaints_per_yr'] = (
    df['complaints_6m'] * 2 /
    (df['tenure_months'] / 12).clip(lower=0.5)
)

This converts the available complaint information into an annualized measure relative to tenure.

It provides a way to compare complaint behaviour across subscribers with different tenure lengths.

Heavy Data User

I also created:

df['is_heavy_data'] = (
    df['avg_monthly_gb'] >
    df['avg_monthly_gb'].quantile(0.75)
).astype(int)

This identifies subscribers whose average monthly data usage is above the 75th percentile.

I learned that binary or banded features can capture useful behavioural patterns that may not be obvious from raw numerical values.

