10. Understanding RMSE

I learned about Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE).

RMSE measures the typical size of prediction errors while giving greater weight to larger errors.

For ARPU prediction, RMSE can be interpreted in terms of rupees, which makes it easier to communicate to a business audience.

For example, an RMSE of ₹40 would indicate that the typical prediction error is on the order of tens of rupees.

The exercise emphasized reporting RMSE in rupees rather than relying only on R².

11. Understanding MAE

I also learned about Mean Absolute Error (MAE).

MAE measures the average absolute difference between the actual ARPU and predicted ARPU.

It provides an easy-to-understand measure of the average prediction error.

Unlike RMSE, MAE does not give as much additional weight to larger errors.

Therefore, comparing RMSE and MAE can provide a better understanding of the model's prediction errors.

12. Understanding R²

I learned that R² (R-squared) measures how much of the variation in the target is explained by the model.

Although R² is useful for evaluating regression performance, the exercise emphasized that it should not be the only metric reported.

A business team is often more interested in questions such as:

How many rupees away from the actual ARPU are our predictions?

This is why RMSE and MAE are important alongside R².

13. Comparing the Regression Models

The two regression models were compared using:

RMSE
MAE
R²

The comparison provided a more complete picture of model performance.

I learned that the model with the highest R² is not automatically the most useful model for every business situation.

The expected RMSE was in the range of tens of rupees compared with an average ARPU in the hundreds.

The exercise also highlighted that an unexpectedly high R², such as above 0.98, can be a warning sign that a leaking feature may still be present.

14. Understanding Residuals

One of the most important concepts in this exercise was residual analysis.

A residual represents the difference between the actual value and the predicted value:

Residual = Actual ARPU - Predicted ARPU

The exercise calculated:

resid = y_te - pred

I learned that residuals can reveal problems that may not be visible from R² alone.

