18. Turning Model Results into Business Recommendations

The exercise required taking the top two revenue drivers and converting them into recommendations for a marketing team.

I learned that the final recommendation should avoid technical machine-learning terminology.

Instead of discussing model parameters, the focus should be on questions such as:

Which customer behaviour is associated with higher revenue?
Which customer segment should the marketing team pay attention to?
What kind of customer behaviour could be explored through campaigns or offers?

This helped me understand how technical model results can be translated into business decisions.

19. Association Does Not Mean Causation

An important learning point was that feature importance shows association, not causation.

For example, if heavy data usage is identified as an important driver, this does not automatically mean that increasing a customer's data usage will cause their ARPU to increase.

Heavy users may already be subscribed to richer or more expensive plans.

Therefore, I learned that model importance should be interpreted carefully and should not automatically be treated as proof that changing a feature will cause revenue to change.

